<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akenzua II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akenzua II was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> from 1933 until his death in 1978. The son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eweka-ii">Eweka II</a>, he continued his father's revival of the monarchy and its court institutions after the British conquest of 1897, and he took part in Nigerian regional politics in the late colonial period. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erediauwa">Erediauwa</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akenzua-ii.jpg" alt="Akenzua II" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eckart von Sydow (1886 - 1942) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oba_Akenzua_II,_1936_0327.0008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
